<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ruđer Bošković</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1711">1711</a>–<a href="/years/1787">1787</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Roger Joseph Boscovich, Ruggero Giuseppe Boscovich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ruđer Bošković was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> physicist, astronomer and mathematician born in Ragusa (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>). Working mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, he proposed an influential atomic theory of point-like centres of force, measured the arc of the meridian in the Papal States and wrote on optics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a>. He was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society in 1761.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ruder-boskovic.jpg" alt="Ruđer Bošković" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rudjer_Boskovic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
